{{nihongo|'''KTK'''|国民通信協会|Kokumin Tsūshin Kyōkai|official Mercian name: '''People's Broadcasting Corporation''' or the '''Kokumin Broadcasting Corporation'''}} is [[Okaiken]]'s national public broadcasting system. Unlike centralized public broadcasters found in many nations, KTK operates under a distinctive '''pillarized association model''' ({{nihongo|柱制放送|hashirasei hōsō|lit. "pillar broadcasting"}}), in which multiple independent broadcasting associations—each representing a different social, ideological, or ethnic community within Okaiken—produce programming for KTK's channels. KTK itself functions as the coordinating umbrella organization, managing the broadcasting infrastructure, allocating airtime among the associations, and operating the channel multiplexes.

KTK operates five terrestrial television channels (KTK:1, the flagship general channel; KTK:2, a culture and arts channel; KTK:E, an educational and children's channel; KTK:News, a 24-hour news channel; and KTK:5, an entertainment and lifestyle channel), three satellite television services (KTK:1BS, KTK:2BS, and KTK:5BS) broadcasting in 4K HDR, and three national radio networks (KTK:R1, KTK:R2, and KTK:RF). Seven broadcasting associations currently hold membership and produce programming for KTK's channels. KTK also provides an international broadcasting service, KTK:World, comprising KTK:W, KTK:W2, and the shortwave radio service KTK:WorldRadio.

The system is funded through a combination of 0.05% of Okaiken's Value Added Tax (VAT), membership fees collected by each broadcasting association, and commercial sponsorship on designated programming. The Broadcasting Act of 1950, as amended, governs the allocation of airtime among associations proportional to their paid membership.

==History==
[[File:KTK logo.svg|200px|thumb|KTK Logo from 1968 – 2017]]

===Early broadcasting (1924–1950)===

KTK's earliest forerunner was the {{nihongo|Okami Broadcasting Station|大紙放送局}} founded in 1924 under the leadership of Count [[Gotō Shinpei]]. Okami Broadcasting Station, along with separate organizations in Toyozawa and Mito, began radio broadcasts in 1925. The three stations merged under the first incarnation of the organization in 1926 as the ''Isolated Broadcasting Network'', which gave it its current name, KTK. KTK was modelled on the RAI of Insulo, and the merger and reorganisation was carried out under the auspices of the pre-war [[Ministry of Communications]]. KTK's second radio network began in 1932; and the third radio network, broadcasting in FM, began in 1937.

KTK began shortwave broadcasting on an experimental, but international broadcasting in the early 1930s by purchasing and controlling frequencies; the company began regular Mercian and Sanese-language shortwave broadcasts in 1935 under the name '''Radio Mitoyagi''', initially aimed at ethnic Sanese listeners in [[Kazan]] and the west coast of [[Lurandia]]. By the late 1930s KTK's overseas broadcasts were known as '''Radio Okami''', which became an official name in 1941.

In November 1947, the [[Imperial Sanese Army]] nationalised all public news agencies and coordinated their efforts via the Information Liaison Confidential Committee. All published and broadcast news reports became official announcements of the Imperial Army General Headquarters in Okami for the duration of the Great Adonian War. The famous [[Okami Rose]] wartime programs were broadcasts by Radio Okami. Radio Okami also broadcast the ''[[Gyokuon-hōsō]]'', the armistice speech made by Emperor [[Wakayama]], in August 1952.

===The Broadcasting Act and the Three Pillars (1950–1964)===

Following the [[Armistice of Okami]] and the transition government under [[Prince Tago Shōka]], the newly formed civilian government confronted the question of how to restructure Okaiken's broadcasting system. The pre-war model—a single, state-controlled broadcaster—was discredited by its role in wartime propaganda. At the same time, Okaiken's society was deeply divided along ideological, ethnic, and class lines. The Imperial loyalists, the resurgent labour movement, the liberal business community, and the Kwangju ethnic minority each demanded a voice in the new broadcasting landscape.

The solution came in the form of the '''Broadcasting Act of 1950''', drafted under the guidance of Information Minister {{nihongo|Suda Shigeyuki|須田重之}}. The Act established KTK as a public umbrella organization that would manage the broadcasting infrastructure and coordinate the activities of independent '''broadcasting associations''' ({{nihongo|放送協会|hōsō kyōkai}}). Each association would represent a distinct social "pillar" ({{nihongo|柱|hashira}}) and would receive airtime on KTK's channels proportional to its paid membership. The model was influenced by the consociational broadcasting systems emerging in Western Illypnia, adapted to Okaiken's unique social fabric.

The first three associations were licensed in 1951:

* The {{nihongo|'''Kokumin Hōsō Renmei'''|国民放送連盟|KHR|lit. "National Broadcasting Alliance"}}, representing the Imperial establishment, traditional Sanese culture, and conservative values. KHR was the successor to the pre-war KTK and inherited much of its staff and facilities.
* The {{nihongo|'''Rōdōsha Hōsō Kyōkai'''|労働者放送協会|RHK|lit. "Workers' Broadcasting Association"}}, representing the labour movement, trade unions, and social-democratic politics.
* The {{nihongo|'''Jiyū Hōsō Renmei'''|自由放送連盟|JHR|lit. "Liberty Broadcasting Alliance"}}, representing the liberal business community, urban professionals, and free-market advocates.

This three-association system—known colloquially as the {{nihongo|"Three Pillars"|三柱|Mitsuhashira}}—defined Okaiken's broadcasting landscape for its first decade. Programming on KTK's single television channel and two radio networks was divided among the three associations, with KHR receiving the largest share due to its dominant membership. KHR's programming included the popular ''[[Taisen drama]]''s beginning in 1961, while RHK launched the long-running ''Onnamono'' dramas in 1957.

KTK's holdings were split into several companies in 1952; Radio Okaiken was made its separate entity on 7 July 1952 while its television broadcasting division was split into two: RMT (Radio Manabi Terebi) and RTK (Radio Terebi Koritsu). RMT focused on educational shows and women-oriented programming, while RTK focused on men-oriented shows, the news and action programming. Both RTK and RMT would merge with Radio Okaiken in 1964 to create the modern KTK under the pillarized framework.

RTK opened the first stage of its current headquarters in [[Okami]] as an international broadcasting center for the [[1960 Summer Adoniad]], the first widely-televised Adonian Games. The complex was gradually expanded through 1973, when it became the headquarters for KTK.

===Expansion and the golden age of pillarization (1958–1968)===

The 1960s saw the greatest expansion of the pillarized system. The Kwangju community, which constituted nearly 20% of Okaiken's population, had long been underserved by the three existing associations. Following sustained advocacy, the {{nihongo|'''Kwangju Hōsō Kyōkai'''|光州放送協会|KHK|lit. "Kwangju Broadcasting Association"}} was licensed in 1958, becoming the first ethnic-minority broadcasting association in Okaiken. KHK's programming, broadcast in both Sanese and Kwangju, was credited with significantly raising the visibility of Kwangju culture and issues in the national discourse.

The {{nihongo|'''Bunka Hōsō Renmei'''|文化放送連盟|BHR|lit. "Culture Broadcasting Alliance"}} was licensed in 1961, representing the arts community, intellectuals, and educational interests. BHR's programming—documentaries, art films, classical music, and avant-garde theatre—became culturally influential despite its relatively small membership.

During this period, the hashira system extended far beyond broadcasting. Each pillar maintained its own schools, hospitals, newspapers, trade unions, and social clubs. The "pillarized" life of Okaiken meant that a conservative Imperial loyalist might attend a KHR-affiliated school, read a KHR-aligned newspaper, join a KHR-linked union, and watch KHR-produced television—living an entire social existence within a single pillar. This parallel social structure, while providing community and identity, also reinforced social divisions.

KTK's second television channel, KTK:2, launched in 1964, primarily featuring BHR and KHK programming. The Educational Television service (now KTK:E) launched in 1959, with programming contributed by all associations. Satellite broadcasting began on KTK BS 1 in 1984, followed by KTK BS 2 in 1985.

===Republican centralization: the PCDS era (1969–2010)===

The [[Second Imperial Restoration (Okaiken)|Imperial Restoration]] of 2011 was preceded by over four decades of republican governance, during which the pillarized broadcasting system was profoundly altered. In 1969, the Federal Republic government dissolved the broadcasting associations and centralized KTK under a new entity, the '''People's Communications and Distribution Service''' ({{nihongo|国民通信配給庁|Kokumin Tsūshin Haikyūchō|PCDS}}). The associations were not formally abolished but were reclassified as "programme production cooperatives" with no guaranteed airtime rights. Airtime allocation was transferred to a government-appointed Programming Board.

The PCDS era was marked by significant controversy. Supporters argued that centralization eliminated the ideological fragmentation of the pillar system and produced a unified national voice. Critics—including the associations themselves, which continued to operate as private production companies—contested that the PCDS was a tool of state propaganda and that the dissolution of the pillar system silenced minority voices, particularly the Kwangju and Atsu communities. KHK's Kwangju-language programming was reduced to a single weekly slot, a decision that provoked protests in Kwangju-majority neighborhoods across Okaiken.

During the PCDS era, the technical infrastructure of broadcasting continued to expand. KTK began satellite broadcasting on the KTK BS 1 channel in 1984, followed by KTK BS 2 in 1985. Satellite broadcasts to Lurandia and Western Fosia began in 1995, which led to the launch of [[KTK World TV]] in 1998. It became [[wikipedia:free-to-air|free-to-air]] over the Astra 19.2°E (Astra 1L) and WAbird satellites in Western Fosia in 2008. In April 2011, the BS 2 channel was re-branded as "BS Premium."

KTK began [[wikipedia:digital television|digital television]] broadcasting in December 2000 on the BS Digital (BShi) station, followed by terrestrial digital TV broadcasts in three major metropolitan areas in 2003. Its digital television coverage gradually expanded to cover almost all of Okaiken by July 2011, when analog transmissions were discontinued.

===Post-Restoration revival and depillarization (2010–present)===

The political negotiations that culminated in the [[Second Imperial Restoration (Okaiken)|Second Imperial Restoration]] of February 2011 included, as a central demand of the associations, the restoration of the pillarized broadcasting system. The '''Broadcasting Restoration Act of 2010''' re-established KTK under its original name, restored the broadcasting associations' airtime rights, and reinstated the membership-based allocation formula. The {{nihongo|'''Atsu Hōsō Kyōkai'''|阿蘇放送協会|AHK|lit. "Atsu Broadcasting Association"}}, which had been denied a license throughout the PCDS era despite years of advocacy, was finally licensed in 2012.

However, the restored system was not a simple return to the 1950s. Okaiken's society had changed fundamentally during four decades of republican rule. The rigid social boundaries of the old pillar system had eroded, and younger Sanese increasingly rejected the idea of organizing their lives around a single ideological community. The {{nihongo|'''Taishū Hōsō Renmei'''|大衆放送連盟|THR|lit. "Mass Broadcasting Alliance"}} was licensed in 2013 as the first "breakthrough" association—unaffiliated with any traditional pillar, THR focused on entertainment, popular culture, and broad-appeal programming. Its rapid membership growth signaled the beginning of {{nihongo|depillarization|柱解体|hashira kaitai}}.

The Broadcasting Act was substantially amended in 2018, lowering the minimum membership threshold for new associations and introducing a "programming quality" criterion alongside the membership-based allocation. The reforms also consolidated KTK's separate news divisions into a single '''KTK News''' division, ensuring neutral and professional news coverage across all channels.

In the 2020s, the associations increasingly cooperated on co-productions, and the ideological distinctiveness of their programming continued to blur. KHR and JHR, both representing centre-right constituencies, were widely seen as redundant; proposals to merge them have been debated since 2020 but have not advanced. RHK, while still formally linked to the labour movement, now produces a broad range of programming including popular drama and entertainment.

Despite depillarization, the system retains its distinctive structure. Each association still maintains its own editorial identity, production facilities, and membership base. The principle that Okaiken's airwaves should reflect the diversity of its society—rather than being controlled by a single state broadcaster or purely commercial interests—remains deeply embedded in Sanese media culture.

The digital broadcasts were later expanded to cover 4K coverage throughout Okaiken, and most programs began to broadcast on this resolution by May 2016. In February 2019, KTK began testing on 4K HDR Resolution, with the coverage of the 2019 Table Tennis Cup being the first broadcast at this resolution. 8K Broadcasting was slated to begin on May 2019, but due to the unavailability of the 4K resolution to the general market, test broadcasts were moved to March 2020.

==The Hashira System==
{{main|Hashira system (Okaiken)}}

The {{nihongo|'''hashira system'''|柱制|hashirasei|lit. "pillar system"}} is the social and institutional framework in which Okaiken's society was historically organized along ideological, ethnic, and class lines into distinct "pillars" ({{nihongo|柱|hashira}}). Each pillar encompassed a complete social ecosystem—schools, hospitals, newspapers, trade unions, social clubs, and, critically, broadcasting associations—that allowed its members to live largely within a single community from cradle to grave.

The hashira system emerged organically in the late 19th and early 20th centuries as Okaiken modernized and different social groups organized to protect their interests. The system was formalized by the Broadcasting Act of 1950, which translated the existing social pillarization into a broadcasting framework.

The five traditional pillars are:

