|-
| {{nihongo|Labor Pillar|労柱|Rōhashira}} || [[Rōdōsha Hōsō Kyōkai|RHK]] || 1951 || Trade unions, workers' movements, social democracy
|-
| {{nihongo|Liberal Pillar|自由柱|Jiyūhashira}} || [[Jiyū Hōsō Renmei|JHR]] || 1951 || Business community, urban professionals, free-market liberalism
|-
| {{nihongo|Kwangju Pillar|光州柱|Kwangjuhashira}} || [[Kwangju Hōsō Kyōkai|KHK]] || 1958 || Kwangju ethnic minority (19.7% of population)
|-
| {{nihongo|Atsu Pillar|阿蘇柱|Atsuhashira}} || [[Atsu Hōsō Kyōkai|AHK]] || 2012 || Atsu indigenous people (3.2% of population)
|-
|}

Two additional associations—[[Bunka Hōsō Renmei|BHR]] (1961) and [[Taishū Hōsō Renmei|THR]] (2013)—are not considered part of the traditional pillar structure. BHR represents cultural and intellectual life rather than a social pillar, while THR was established as a post-pillar, entertainment-focused association.

Depillarization has significantly eroded the social boundaries between pillars since the 1990s, though the broadcasting associations remain as institutional legacies of the system. Surveys consistently show that a majority of Sanese under 40 cannot identify which pillar their family traditionally belonged to, and membership in the associations is increasingly driven by programming preferences rather than ideological alignment.

==Broadcasting Associations==

KTK's programming is produced by seven licensed broadcasting associations, each of which maintains its own editorial staff, production facilities, and membership base. Airtime on KTK's channels is allocated among the associations based on paid membership numbers, subject to minimum thresholds set by the Broadcasting Act.

