==Governance and Funding==

KTK is governed by a '''Board of Directors''' appointed by the Minister of Communications on the advice of the {{nihongo|'''Broadcasting Council'''|放送審議会|Hōsō Shingikai}}, which includes representatives of each licensed association, independent members, and government observers. The Director-General, currently Masayuki Matsumoto, serves as KTK's chief executive and is responsible for the day-to-day management of the organization.

===Funding===

KTK's funding comes from three sources:
* '''VAT allocation''': 0.05% of Okaiken's Value Added Tax is allocated to KTK for infrastructure, transmission, and coordination costs.
* '''Membership fees''': Each broadcasting association collects annual membership fees from its members. A portion of these fees is transferred to KTK to fund programming production. The fee is set by each association independently, but the Broadcasting Act mandates a minimum fee of 円2,000 per year.
* '''Commercial sponsorship''': Designated programming on KTK:1 and KTK:5 may carry commercial sponsorship, subject to restrictions on the type and frequency of advertisements. KTK:2, KTK:E, and KTK:News are advertisement-free.

===Airtime allocation===

Airtime on KTK's channels is allocated among the associations based on a formula set out in the Broadcasting Act. The primary factor is paid membership, but the Act also includes provisions for:
* A '''minimum airtime guarantee''' for each licensed association, ensuring that even the smallest associations (BHR, AHK) receive meaningful airtime.
* A '''news allocation''' reserved for [[KTK News]], the centralized news division, which produces neutral news programming across all channels.
* A '''quality bonus''' introduced in the 2018 amendments, which provides additional airtime to associations that produce award-winning or critically acclaimed programming.

